================================================================================================
S1. THE SECANT CURVATURE PAIR
================================================================================================
  R1 states: 'No L-BFGS curvature pair (y_k = g_{k+1} - g_k) is reconstructible from any
  completed run ... Buying curvature as fresh gradients is FORCED, not chosen, which is what
  prices the whole Bayesian line.'  True of CONSECUTIVE-ITERATE pairs. Not true of SECANT
  pairs: the driver keeps eval 1 and every 10th evaluation (invert_lbfgsb.py:96-97,120-121),
  so a run that reached evaluation 10 archived a matched (beta, grad) pair at TWO states.

  Availability audit:
    S1-cbfs-inversion      ['beta_eval010.npy', 'grad_eval001.npy', 'grad_eval010.npy'] -> SECANT PAIR
    S1-cbfs-reinversion    ['beta_eval010.npy', 'grad_eval001.npy', 'grad_eval010.npy'] -> SECANT PAIR
    S1-cbfs-weighted-arm   ['grad_eval001.npy']                                       -> no pair

  Frame: state A = evaluation 1 (beta == 1 exactly, C1 above); state B = evaluation 10
  (beta_eval010 / grad_eval010, the matched pair D9 established). s = beta_10 - 1.

  --- S1-cbfs-reinversion (repaired)  (n=21000)
      ||s||                                    = 4.312344e+00
      s^T y_penalty  (IDENTITY 2*lambda_L2*||s||^2) = +3.719261e-04  (0.05% of s^T y_total)
      s^T y_QoI      (the MEASUREMENT)         = +7.313133e-01  (99.95% of s^T y_total)
      R_qoi   = s^T y_QoI / ||s||^2            = +3.932573e-02
      R_prior = 2*lambda_L2 (every direction)  = +2.000000e-05
      PRIOR-PRECONDITIONED RAYLEIGH QUOTIENT   = +1.966287e+03

  --- S1-cbfs-inversion (corrupted)  (n=21000)
      ||s||                                    = 2.391395e+00
      s^T y_penalty  (IDENTITY 2*lambda_L2*||s||^2) = +1.143754e-03  (62.17% of s^T y_total)
      s^T y_QoI      (the MEASUREMENT)         = +6.960815e-04  (37.83% of s^T y_total)
      R_qoi   = s^T y_QoI / ||s||^2            = +1.217187e-04
      R_prior = 2*lambda_L2 (every direction)  = +2.000000e-04
      PRIOR-PRECONDITIONED RAYLEIGH QUOTIENT   = +6.085935e-01

  --- S1-cbfs-reinversion (repaired)  [FREE CELLS]  (n=20995)
      ||s||                                    = 3.923813e+00
      s^T y_penalty  (IDENTITY 2*lambda_L2*||s||^2) = +3.079261e-04  (0.05% of s^T y_total)
      s^T y_QoI      (the MEASUREMENT)         = +6.010683e-01  (99.95% of s^T y_total)
      R_qoi   = s^T y_QoI / ||s||^2            = +3.903977e-02
      R_prior = 2*lambda_L2 (every direction)  = +2.000000e-05
      PRIOR-PRECONDITIONED RAYLEIGH QUOTIENT   = +1.951989e+03

  IDENTITY vs MEASUREMENT. s^T y_penalty = 2*lambda_L2*||s||^2 is derivable by construction
  from (beta, lambda_L2): any treatment knowing those reproduces it exactly, including one
  whose curvature is wrong. Reported, never gated on. s^T y_QoI needs grad_eval010.npy --
  an adjoint solve no closed form supplies -- and is a measurement.

  NULLS, stated before the numbers:
    N1 flat objective (H_qoi = 0)                  -> s^T y_QoI = 0, rho = 0
    N2 the two gradients are the same array        -> identical to N1
    N3 sign-randomised y_QoI, 200 draws            -> E[s^T y_QoI] = 0 (chance level)
    reinversion  N3 null: mean -7.489e-03 sd 1.120e-01 | MEASURED +7.313e-01  (6.6 sd from null)
    inversion    N3 null: mean +1.084e-06 sd 6.958e-05 | MEASURED +6.961e-04  (10.0 sd from null)

  NEGATIVE CONTROL -- cross-pairing s from one run with y from the other:
    s(reinv)^T y(inv)/||s||^2  = +1.288737e-04   vs matched reinversion R_qoi = +3.932573e-02
    s(inv)^T y(reinv)/||s||^2  = +1.764017e-02   vs matched inversion   R_qoi = +1.217187e-04

================================================================================================
S1b. OUT-OF-SAMPLE CONTROL -- does the secant curvature predict the OBJECTIVE HISTORY?
================================================================================================
  J_history_main.csv is written from solver stdout and shares no array with
  grad_eval*.npy. A quadratic model built from (g1, s, y) is scored against it.

  S1-cbfs-reinversion
    actual J(eval10)-J(eval1)          = -0.562398
    linear only  g^T s                 = -0.758439   (34.86% error)
    linear + 0.5 s^T y (SECANT)        = -0.392597   (30.19% error)
  S1-cbfs-inversion
    actual J(eval10)-J(eval1)          = -0.000921
    linear only  g^T s                 = -0.001841   (99.98% error)
    linear + 0.5 s^T y (SECANT)        = -0.000921   (0.06% error)

  A quadratic is exact only if J is quadratic; the residual is the cubic-and-higher
  remainder over the step actually taken. What the check establishes is that s^T y carries
  the sign and the magnitude needed to close the gap the linear term leaves -- which a
  mismatched or spurious y could not do.

================================================================================================
S1c. HEAVY-TAIL DISCIPLINE
================================================================================================
    reinversion  |g(eval1)| vs |g(eval10)|: Spearman +0.9741  Pearson +0.6449  (max/median 2.05e+06)
    inversion    |g(eval1)| vs |g(eval10)|: Spearman +0.9976  Pearson +0.8421  (max/median 1.14e+07)
    SPEARMAN carries every claim in this document. Both gradient fields have
    max/median above 1e6; a Pearson coefficient over that dynamic range is a statement
    about a few hundred extreme cells and moves with tail shape. Pearson is reported
    because the lab asks for both, and is not relied on.

================================================================================================
S2. G-P4 IS DEGENERATE -- all three legs are functions of (beta, g_QoI, lambda_L2)
================================================================================================
  Recomputed from beta_eval010.npy + grad_eval010.npy (S1-cbfs-inversion):
    ratio r = |g_pen|/|g_QoI| = 0.998441   (gate target 0.998441)
    cos(g_QoI, -g_pen)        = 0.999542   (gate target 0.999542)
    rms|beta-1|               = 0.016502   (gate target 0.016502)
    ||g_total||               = 1.450369e-05 (J_history row 10 prints 1.450e-05)
    eps = ||g_total||/|g_QoI| = 3.027748e-02

  LEG BY LEG -- what does a treatment need in order to reproduce each number?
    rms|beta-1| : a function of BETA ALONE, and beta is an INPUT to the gate.
    ratio r     : 2*lambda_L2*||beta-1|| / ||g_QoI||. Numerator is the identity the
                  withdrawal removed; the only new content is the scalar ||g_QoI||.
    cosine c    : fixed by the other two through eps^2 = 1 + r^2 - 2*r*c. Solving:
                    c from (r, eps) = 0.999542138470174
                    c measured      = 0.999542138470174   (relative agreement 2.2e-16)

  THE FAILURE MODE, EXHIBITED. Treatment W reports the PRIOR as the posterior: covariance
  = prior covariance, credible intervals straddling beta = 1 in 100% of cells, rank 0. It is
  the exact shape falsifier F1 exists to catch. Handed the archived beta and re-evaluating
  the same g_QoI, its G-P4 is:
    ratio 0.998441  cos 0.999542  rms 0.016502  -> agreement with the targets 0.0e+00. G-P4: PASS.
    None of the three legs is a function of any covariance, any credible interval or
    any Hessian, so G-P4 cannot see the defect. It grades the GRADIENT, not the posterior --
    structurally the same finding as 'G2 scores the adjoint, not the closure'.

  AND THE COSINE IS NEAR 1 FOR A REASON UNRELATED TO THE PRIOR BEING RIGHT. At any interior
  stationary point g_QoI + g_pen = 0, hence r = c = 1 exactly. The plateau is stationary to
  within eps, so
    c >= 1 - eps^2/(2r) = 0.999540922 is forced by convergence alone; measured c = 0.999542138.
    The published 0.9995 is 'the run converged', restated as an angle.

  WHAT G-P4 DOES TEST, so a replacement can keep it: exactly two scalars -- ||g_QoI|| at the
  plateau state and the angle between g_QoI and (beta-1). That is a sound REPRODUCTION
  control on the gradient and would catch the mismatched-evaluation error that produced the
  1.684x confusion. It is not, and cannot be, a control on a posterior.

================================================================================================
S3. PR-1 SETTLED FROM DISK -- the second-QoI sensitivity map already exists
================================================================================================
  PR-1 (S1_SENSITIVITY_VS_ERROR.md S7) asks for 25 core-min to buy 'a second-QoI
  sensitivity map on CBFS at beta = 1', and pre-registers the decision rule:
  'If the second map's geography matches the first, reading 2 is confirmed outright and
   R5/R8 are closed.'

  The map is already on disk. S1-cbfs-weighted-arm/cbfs_inv/grad_anchorw.npy is
  dJw/dbeta at beta = 1, where Jw = 5319*varUwin + 3591*varUrec -- two disjoint boxToCell
  variances over 2,970 cells (runScript_w.py:79-105), a DIFFERENT functional from the
  all-cells varianceU. It cost 16.83 core-min and was billed on 2026-08-08.

  PROVENANCE of grad_anchorw, three ways:
    (a) runScript_w.py:144 sets b0 = np.ones(NCELLS) unless -betafile is given;
        log.anchorw carries no -betafile.
    (b) driver_w.py:24 comments LQOI = 1/27.465931825190644 = '1 / Jw_raw(beta=1),
        anchorw', and log.anchorw:18720 prints OBJ Jw: 2.7465931825190644e+01.
    (c) log.anchorw:18721 prints GRAD norm 7.3228773523e+00; measured here 7.3228773523e+00
    ledger.csv: anchorw rc=0 wall=505 core_min=16.83
    DV order control: weighted-arm dv_to_serial_perm == reinversion's: True

  OBJECTIVE 1: varianceU over ALL 21,000 cells (grad_eval001, reinversion)
    G2 on this map (bar >50%): 35.3810%
      in-window 0<=x<=6,0<=y<=2     35.38%  (base  8.44%, enrichment x4.19)
      upstream x<0                  45.76%  (base 32.14%, enrichment x1.42)
      above shear layer y>2          9.76%  (base 69.60%, enrichment x0.14)
      downstream x>6 & y<=2         12.52%  (base 13.60%, enrichment x0.92)
      ON the W2 support             42.33%  (base 14.14%, enrichment x2.99)

  OBJECTIVE 2: Jw over 2,970 cells in two boxes (grad_anchorw, weighted arm)
    G2 on this map (bar >50%): 36.5238%
      in-window 0<=x<=6,0<=y<=2     36.52%  (base  8.44%, enrichment x4.33)
      upstream x<0                  45.57%  (base 32.14%, enrichment x1.42)
      above shear layer y>2          4.48%  (base 69.60%, enrichment x0.06)
      downstream x>6 & y<=2         15.29%  (base 13.60%, enrichment x1.12)
      ON the W2 support             45.33%  (base 14.14%, enrichment x3.21)

  DO THE TWO MAPS AGREE?
    Spearman(|g_varU|, |g_Jw|) = +0.9894   [carries the claim]
    Pearson (|g_varU|, |g_Jw|) = +0.9992   [tail-dominated, reported not relied on]
    top-decile set overlap = 1975 of 2100 = 94.05%   (chance 10%)

    For scale, R1's OWN 'second independent baseline gradient' (same objective,
    different inlet): Spearman +0.9450 (R1 published +0.9450), top-decile overlap 61.14%
    Changing the objective's support by 7x moves the geography LESS than repairing
    the inlet did.

  THE IDENTITY HAZARD, STATED. Jw is supported on the two boxes, so a high IN-WINDOW share
  of its top decile is partly derivable by construction from the mask and is not gated on.
  The leg that is NOT derivable is the share OFF the support, reachable only by adjoint
  propagation, and the upstream x<0 share in particular -- no box touches x<0.
    varianceU (obj 1)    top decile OFF the W2 support  57.67% | upstream x<0  45.76%
    Jw        (obj 2)    top decile OFF the W2 support  54.67% | upstream x<0  45.57%
    The two upstream shares agree to 0.19 percentage points.

